id: rust.blocking-in-async
kind: rust
title: Blocking in async code
aliases: spawn_blocking, blocking call, cpu bound
tags: async, performance, pitfalls
questions:
  - what happens if you call a blocking function inside an async task?
  - how do you run CPU-bound work in Tokio?
invariant: A blocking call on a runtime worker stalls every task scheduled on that thread, so blocking work belongs in spawn_blocking or a dedicated pool.
answer_30s: Blocking inside async starves the runtime: use spawn_blocking for file IO, hashing and CPU work, and never hold a std lock or sleep synchronously in a task.
answer_2m: |
  The runtime has roughly one worker per core, and a blocked worker cannot poll
  other tasks, so a 50ms synchronous call inside a handler adds latency to
  unrelated requests sharing that thread. The toolkit: spawn_blocking for
  blocking IO and CPU-bound work, tokio::fs instead of std::fs, and a bounded
  blocking pool for heavy jobs. Related traps: std::thread::sleep instead of
  tokio::time::sleep, and holding a std MutexGuard across an await. Detectable
  symptom: latency that scales with concurrency while CPU looks idle.
when_to_use:
  - hashing, compression, image work, blocking SDKs and legacy sync libraries
avoid_when:
  - tiny pure-CPU operations where the hand-off costs more than the work
minimal_example: |
  let digest = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {
      sha256_of_bytes(&payload) // CPU-bound, off the runtime
  })
  .await?;
  let _ = digest;
failure_modes:
  - std::fs or a synchronous client inside a handler
  - std::thread::sleep where async sleep was intended
  - unbounded spawn_blocking, which only moves the queue
related:
  - rust.tokio-spawn
  - rust.mutex-across-await
  - design.bounded-queue
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/task/fn.spawn_blocking.html
